# Only the built site is deployed.
#
# This repository is an Expo app plus a static marketing site, and the Vercel
# project has buildCommand: null with outputDirectory: website/dist. Everything
# below is either already copied into dist or irrelevant to a static site, so
# uploading it would just slow the deploy down.
#
# The most important line is website/public/downloads: the universal APK is
# 110.1 MB and a Vercel Hobby deployment caps static uploads at 100 MB. The APK
# is served from a GitHub release instead, so the binary must never be uploaded.

# The APK (hosted on a GitHub release; see website/site.config.mjs apkPath)
website/public/downloads
website/dist/downloads

# Build output is generated from these; dist already contains the copies.
website/public/assets
website/public/*.png
website/src
website/*.mjs
website/README.md

# Staging directory for the web dashboard export. Never deployed: dist/app is the
# copy that ships. Listed because an interrupted build can leave it behind, and it
# is a full ~7 MB export of the app bundle.
website/.dashboard-build

# The Expo application itself is not part of a static marketing site.
#
# These MUST stay anchored with a leading slash. `.vercelignore` uses gitignore
# syntax, where a bare name matches that directory at ANY depth. Unanchored, the
# `assets` line below also matched website/dist/assets (every stylesheet, script,
# font and image) and website/dist/app/assets (the dashboard's Inter fonts and
# expo-router icons), so the upload shipped a site whose HTML loaded but whose
# entire assets tree was missing -- the page rendered raw and unstyled.
/src
/assets
/images
/supabase
/scripts
/docs
/.expo
/ios
/android
/.expo-shared

# Tooling / CI noise
.github
.claude
*.log
*.tsbuildinfo